# Car Underwriter (local prototype)

This is a local, single-vehicle underwriting prototype:
- Captures Copart listing evidence (supervised browser).
- Downloads gallery photos into the case `photos/` folder when possible.
- Optionally queries eBay for parts candidates (requires your own approved credentials).
- Runs an AI-assisted inspection + provisional budget + bid ceiling calculation.
- Saves an underwriting report (markdown + JSON).

No automatic bidding is performed or approved by this tool.

## 0) Prerequisites

- Windows + Python 3.11+ (`py -3`)
- A working Copart UK login in a normal browser
- Optional: eBay production credentials (Browse API) if you want live parts searches

## 1) One-time setup

Create and activate a venv, then install dependencies:

```powershell
py -3 -m venv .venv
.venv\Scripts\activate
python -m pip install -r requirements.txt
python -m playwright install chromium
```

Set your environment in `.env`:
- `OPENAI_API_KEY`
- `OPENAI_MODEL` (defaults to `gpt-4.1`)
- Optional: `EBAY_CLIENT_ID`, `EBAY_CLIENT_SECRET`

Sanity check the financial engine:

```powershell
py -3 finance.py
```

## 1b) UI (recommended)

The UI lets you enter a lot number, edit `settings.json`, and run capture/WBAC/analyse from buttons.
It also supports a simple manual "comparables" list to set exit prices (preferred over WBAC).
It includes an "Auto Trader (offline)" importer that extracts listing URLs/prices from an HTML file you saved in your browser.

Start Chrome with a CDP port (so the UI can reuse your logged-in session):

```powershell
start chrome --remote-debugging-port=9222 --user-data-dir="%LOCALAPPDATA%\\Temp\\copart-debug-profile"
```

Then run:

```powershell
streamlit run ui.py
```

## 2) Underwrite a single Copart lot (end-to-end)

Pick a lot URL:

```text
https://www.copart.co.uk/lot/<LOTNUMBER>
```

### Option A (recommended): drive an existing browser session (CDP)

This reuses your already-logged-in browser session, which helps with Imperva checks.

1) Close all Chrome windows.
2) Start Chrome with a CDP port:

```powershell
start chrome --remote-debugging-port=9222 --user-data-dir="%LOCALAPPDATA%\Temp\copart-debug-profile"
```

3) In that Chrome window: log in to Copart, pass any security check, then open the lot page.
4) Run capture:

```powershell
python underwriter.py capture "https://www.copart.co.uk/lot/<LOTNUMBER>" --case "cases/<LOTNUMBER>" --cdp http://127.0.0.1:9222
```

Tip: if the VRN/registration is masked (e.g. `**66***`), click it on the lot page to reveal it before capturing.

If the photos were not downloaded, run:

```powershell
python underwriter.py collect-photos --case "cases/<LOTNUMBER>" --cdp http://127.0.0.1:9222
```

### Option B: supervised capture with a fresh automated browser

```powershell
python underwriter.py capture "https://www.copart.co.uk/lot/<LOTNUMBER>" --case "cases/<LOTNUMBER>" --browser chrome
```

If you get stuck on an Imperva "Additional security check is required" page:
- Complete the check in the opened browser window, then press Enter again in the terminal.
- Ad/tracker blockers (extensions or system-wide) can prevent the checkbox from rendering.

### Option C (fallback): manual capture

Use this if automation can't render the gallery/challenge reliably.

```powershell
python underwriter.py capture-manual "https://www.copart.co.uk/lot/<LOTNUMBER>" --case "cases/<LOTNUMBER>"
```

Then:
- Paste the full lot details text into `cases/<LOTNUMBER>/listing.txt`
- Put the original vehicle images into `cases/<LOTNUMBER>/photos/`
- Continue with the settings + analysis steps below

## 3) Fill the evidence-backed settings (required before analysis)

Open `cases/<LOTNUMBER>/settings.json` and set these from evidence (not guesses):

- Some fields are prefilled from `listing.txt` when detectable (e.g. `hammer_vat_rate=0` when VAT is stated as not added, and WBAC registration/mileage if unmasked).
- `transport_including_vat`: your real delivery quote (GBP)
- `hammer_vat_rate`: `0` or `0.20` based on the listing VAT status
- `expected_gallery_images`: should match the actual number of vehicle photos
- `photos_complete_confirmed`: set `true` only after you confirm completeness/relevance
- `ulez`: `YES` / `NO` / `UNKNOWN`
- `ulez_evidence`: what checker you used + timestamp
- `wbac.*`: your evidenced WBAC "declared Cat N" exit value
  - If you have a screenshot for audit, put it inside the case folder and set `wbac.evidence_file` to that filename (optional).

### Optional: capture a WBAC valuation screenshot + extract the £ value

If you want the tool to capture your on-screen WBAC valuation (from your own browser session), use:

```powershell
python underwriter.py capture-wbac --case "cases/<LOTNUMBER>" --cdp http://127.0.0.1:9222
```

This saves `wbac_capture.json` + a screenshot file in the case folder, and best-effort extracts a candidate `wbac.value`.
You must still review/confirm Cat N declaration and repaired-condition basis in `settings.json`.

## 4) Run the underwriting analysis

This will send `listing.txt` and the `photos/` images to the configured AI API (costs money).

```powershell
python underwriter.py analyse --case "cases/<LOTNUMBER>" --consent-ai
```

## 5) Review outputs

Look in `cases/<LOTNUMBER>/` for:
- `inspection.json` (vehicle extraction + photo findings)
- `parts.json` (eBay candidates or explicit credential/API errors)
- `budget.json` (low/base/adverse provisional allowances)
- `report.md` (human-readable underwriting summary)
- `report.json` (full evidence-backed record)

If `report.md` says "BLOCKED pending evidence", fix the missing items listed under "Missing evidence" and re-run `analyse`.
